  <id>n8n_sync</id>
  <name>n8n Sync</name>
  <summary>Your n8n workflows as native, editable, backed-up Nextcloud files</summary>
  <description><![CDATA[
n8n Sync surfaces your n8n workflows as native Nextcloud files. Map one or more n8n
tags to folders and every tagged workflow shows up as a `.n8n` file — with a real
file icon, deep links back to n8n, and a per-folder sync mode.

Create, read, update and delete a workflow from either side. Make a file in a mapped
folder and a real workflow appears in n8n; tag a workflow in n8n and its file appears
in the folder. Moving, copying, renaming, trashing and restoring all mirror across,
and a trashed workflow is archived rather than destroyed — nothing is permanently
deleted until you empty the trash.

- **Sync** — full two-way ownership. Edit a workflow in the Files app, over WebDAV,
  or from your desktop client and the change pushes back to n8n; a sync folder
  doubles as a complete, restorable backup of every workflow in it.
- **Link** — a lightweight pointer (id, name, URL) that opens the workflow in n8n.

Tags are kept as one set across all three surfaces — n8n, the file's own `tags` array,
and Nextcloud's searchable system tags — so the mirror is as searchable as n8n itself,
and you can re-tag a workflow from whichever side you happen to be looking at.

Each mirrored file also carries its workflow's own created and modified dates rather
than the sync's, so Nextcloud's own views — Recent, Popular files, sorting a folder by
date — tell you the truth about your automation.

Sync is reconcile-based: re-running a pull never duplicates files, because the link
between a file and its workflow is a stable id stored in the file's metadata — not
the filename — so renaming, moving, and restoring never break the connection.

This is a community integration and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored
by n8n GmbH. "n8n" and the n8n logo are trademarks of n8n GmbH, used here only to
identify the service this app integrates with.
  ]]></description>
